We notice that $t$ can be written as $t=\frac{AB^2}{AC^2\times BC^2}=\frac{AC^2+BC^2}{AC^2}
+\frac{AC^2+BC^2}{BC^2}-2=2+\dfrac{BC^2}{AC^2}+\dfrac{AC^2}{BC^2}.$</think> On the parabola $y=x^2,$ for any line intersecting at $(a,b),(c,d)$ say, the slope of the line is $\dfrac{b-d}{a-c}=2a.\:** Therefore, we can look for any point with slope $2a$ (this is the slope at $x=a$). Then, if the equation of the line is $y=2ax+b,$ we get that the intersection of the line with the parabola is given by $x^2=2ax+b\Rightarrow x^2-2ax-b=0,\:x=\dfrac{2a\pm\sqrt{4a^2+4b}}{2}=a\pm\sqrt{a^2+b}$ $2a^2+2b=a^2+b,\:b=\dfrac{-a^2}{2}$, so that the roots of $x^2-2ax+b=0$ are $x=a\pm i\sqrt{\dfrac{a^2}{2}}.$ Hence we can have roots $a\pm ia;$ if we take $a=1,$ so we have $1\pm i.$ In coordinates, we have $1+i$ and $1-i.$</think> We know that $\left(a+a-b\right)\left(a-a+b\right)\left(a-ia-b\right)\left(a+ia-b\right)=a(a-b)(a^2-\left(a+i\right)^2)(a^2-\left(a-i\right)^2)$ $=\left(a^2+3iab\right)\left(a^2-3iab\right)=\left(a^2+3a^2\right)\left(a^2-3a^2\right)=-8a^4.$ Alternatively, we can just say, as User already said, $AC(x)\cdot BC(x)=AC(x-1)\cdot BC(x-1)=\left(c-x^2\right)\left(c+b\right)=\left(c-b\right)\left(-1+x^2\right).$ [asy]
unitsize(1 cm);

real parab (real x) {
  return(x^2);
}

